{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012yTf4wb2JG5ucHkZwbVv28","type":"tool_result","content":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Scoring Dimensions\n\n## 1. Reviewer Confidence (5-point scale)\n\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## 2. Soundness (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## 3. Excitement (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\nEvaluates subjective appeal: whether papers present breakthroughs, challenge assumptions, impact communities, or inspire reviewer's own work.\n\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others.\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points.\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n\n## 4. Overall Assessment (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n## Additional Dimensions\n\nThe form also covers: limitations/societal impact, ethical concerns, reproducibility (5-point scale), datasets (5-point scale), software (5-point scale), and author anonymity detection (5-point scale)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79301dc3-a43c-42c1-b7ac-01c347c8ab59","uuid":"24f3a5d3-3b92-4813-bab5-d3045680ef2b","timestamp":"2026-05-15T10:23:33.038Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":25365,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# ACL Rolling Review Form - Scoring Dimensions\n\n## 1. Reviewer Confidence (5-point scale)\n\n- **5**: \"Positive that my evaluation is correct. I read the paper very carefully and am familiar with related work.\"\n- **4**: \"Quite sure. I tried to check the important points carefully.\"\n- **3**: \"Pretty sure, but there's a chance I missed something.\"\n- **2**: \"Willing to defend my evaluation, but it is fairly likely that I missed some details.\"\n- **1**: \"Not my area, or paper is very hard to understand.\"\n\n## 2. Soundness (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\n- **5**: \"Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type.\"\n- **4**: \"Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims.\"\n- **3**: \"Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n- **2**: \"Poor: Some of the main claims are not sufficiently supported.\"\n- **1**: \"Major Issues: This study is not yet sufficiently thorough to warrant publication.\"\n\n## 3. Excitement (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\nEvaluates subjective appeal: whether papers present breakthroughs, challenge assumptions, impact communities, or inspire reviewer's own work.\n\n- **5**: \"Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation.\"\n- **4**: \"Exciting: I would mention this paper to others.\"\n- **3**: \"Interesting: I might mention some points.\"\n- **2**: \"Potentially Interesting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n- **1**: \"Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me.\"\n\n## 4. Overall Assessment (5-point scale with half-increments)\n\n- **5**: Consider for Award (top 2.5%)\n- **4**: Conference acceptance\n- **3**: Findings acceptance\n- **2**: Resubmit next cycle\n- **1**: Do not resubmit\n\n## Additional Dimensions\n\nThe form also covers: limitations/societal impact, ethical concerns, reproducibility (5-point scale), datasets (5-point scale), software (5-point scale), and author anonymity detection (5-point scale).","durationMs":6902,"url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KyDBTmBzXw31bceCxxJVb8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form with the scoring rubric. The key dimensions are:\n\n1. **Soundness**: 1-5 (with half-increments)\n2. **Excitement**: 1-5 (with half-increments)\n3. **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 (with half-increments), where:\n   - 5 = Award consideration (top 2.5%)\n   - 4 = Conference acceptance\n   - 3 = Findings acceptance\n   - 2 = Resubmit next cycle\n   - 1 = Do not resubmit\n4. **Reviewer Confidence**: 1-5\n\nThe target is EMNLP 2026 Findings, which maps to an Overall score of 3. Now I'm setting up the reviewer assignments—R1 will focus on methods and experiments while R2 evaluates novelty and related work—then running through the full roundtable process starting with independent reviews, followed by cross-sharing, convergence, and finally synthesis.\n\nI have everything I need to dispatch both reviewers in parallel: the paper itself, the review context with expected experimental outcomes, and the ARR scoring rubric.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_read_input_tokens":54498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79301dc3-a43c-42c1-b7ac-01c347c8ab59","uuid":"4aba6269-c297-4571-822c-1ccb9786ef5c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KyDBTmBzXw31bceCxxJVb8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the ARR review form scoring rubric. Now dispatching R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work) in parallel for independent reviews."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_read_input_tokens":54498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79301dc3-a43c-42c1-b7ac-01c347c8ab59","uuid":"a2830521-4f9d-4b0d-8e02-36be59572e6a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KyDBTmBzXw31bceCxxJVb8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014iVPutYJse2xjGNVzCMbS7","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind peer review of a paper submitted to EMNLP 2026 Findings. Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Scoring Rubric (ARR Review Form)\n\n**Soundness** (1-5, half-increments):\n- 5: One of the most thorough studies I've seen\n- 4: Sufficient support for all claims\n- 3: Sufficient support for main claims\n- 2: Some main claims not sufficiently supported\n- 1: Not yet sufficiently thorough\n\n**Excitement** (1-5, half-increments):\n- 5: Would recommend to others / attend presentation\n- 4: Would mention to others\n- 3: Might mention some points\n- 2: Doesn't resonate\n- 1: Not exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment** (1-5, half-increments):\n- 5: Award (top 2.5%)\n- 4: Conference acceptance\n- 3: Findings acceptance\n- 2: Resubmit next cycle\n- 1: Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Why Confidence Signals Predict but Cannot Select in Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper studies test-time scaling (multi-sample generation + selection) for structured IE (NER, RE). It evaluates 5 confidence signals (log-prob, set Jaccard, Fleiss' kappa, exact match, entity voting) for quality estimation (QE) and best-of-N selection on fine-tuned 8B models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) with constrained decoding (Outlines) on SciERC and CoNLL-2003.\n\n**Key findings:**\n1. **Correlation–selection gap**: Signals correlate with quality (EM AUROC 0.749) but fail to select better than greedy (best Δ = +0.6pp, p=0.39).\n2. **Free-form decoding ablation**: Removing grammar constraints increases log-prob variance (median range 0.03→0.07) but doesn't improve selection (NER Δ=+0.3pp p=0.42; RE Δ=−0.3pp p=0.65). Refutes \"constrained decoding bottleneck\" hypothesis.\n3. **Cross-model validation**: QE signal rankings consistent across Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL-2003 (|Δρ| ≤ 0.021).\n4. **OOD robustness**: Under domain shift, all signals degrade; log-prob becomes anti-correlated (ρ=−0.30).\n5. **Supervised verifier**: DeBERTa-v3-large captures only 6.1% oracle headroom (NER) and 0% (RE).\n6. **Structural explanation**: IE's combinatorial output space means scalar signals can't rank set-valued outputs.\n\n**Tables present:**\n- Table 1: QE results on SciERC (5 signals × ρ + AUROC for NER/RE)\n- Table 2: Best-of-N selection F1 (oracle, 5 signals, greedy, random)\n- Table 3: Parse rate and selection bias (constrained vs free-form)\n- Table 4: Constrained vs free-form detailed comparison\n- Table 5: Cross-model QE on CoNLL-2003\n- Table 6: OOD signal degradation\n- Table 7: Supervised verifier vs unsupervised signals\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (planned experiments)\n\nThe full experiment plan included 4 experiments:\n1. **exp-018 (freeform_decoding_ablation)**: Remove constrained decoding, compare constrained vs free-form. Parse rate ~72% NER, ~65% RE. Parseable subset biased toward easy instances.\n2. **exp-017 (ner_llama_conll)**: LLaMA-3.1-8B on CoNLL-2003 for cross-model validation.\n3. **exp-011 (ner_ood_robustness)**: OOD evaluation under cross-domain transfer.\n4. **exp-019 (simple_verifier_baseline)**: Supervised DeBERTa verifier on (input, output, F1) triples, 5-fold CV.\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Methods and Experiments**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are experiments well-designed? Are controls appropriate? Is the evaluation protocol sound?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are significance tests appropriate? Are effect sizes reported? Are confidence intervals given?\n3. **Methodological concerns**: Any confounds? Missing baselines? Questionable decisions?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are datasets/models standard?\n5. **Coverage**: Are the planned experiments adequately covered in the paper?\n\nStructure your review as:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Paper Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths (bulleted)\n\n### Weaknesses (bulleted)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5  \n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a negative-result paper—evaluate whether the negative results are convincingly established, not whether positive results were obtained."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39,"cache_creation_input_tokens":961,"cache_read_input_tokens":54498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":961},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"79301dc3-a43c-42c1-b7ac-01c347c8ab59","uuid":"d7a8c36f-87e2-4d3e-a511-d4b0a44b0a53"}
